Jira-side edit ... before the next pass; (3) assert the next pass emits rebar's body ... and records the deduped outbound-field-conflict alert' — no index-settle wait is specified","outbound_field_diff.py:456-464 gates the conflict append on `not _remote_matches_baseline(...)`, so a stale remote (Jira-side edit not yet indexed) suppresses the alert","outbound_fields.py:300-307 `_prev_jira_matches`/remote-matches-baseline returns True when the fetched Jira value has not changed — i.e. when the index has not caught up","The existing harness this plan extends treats index lag as its central hazard: `_wait_until_search_reflects` (test_dc_mutations.py:77-109), `_wait_until_parent_map_reflects`, `_wait_until_links_reflect`, each documenting 'This is NOT a bridge defect — the write succeeded, the SEARCH cannot see it yet'"],"finding":"The live second-pass echo-safety and both-sides-conflict assertions have a hidden ordering assumption: they run a reconcile pass and read state without any wait-until-the-index-reflects gate, but the differ reads the remote from an eventually-consistent JQL-search-backed fetch. A pass fired before Jira's Lucene index reflects the landed rich body (echo case) or the concurrent Jira-side edit (conflict case) reads a stale remote — for the conflict case `_remote_matches_baseline` then returns True, no (jira_key, field) is appended to conflict_sink, and the `outbound-field-conflict` alert never fires, failing the AC for a timing reason rather than a bridge defect.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f478e5b82a1b355dd","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach (2nd-reconcile echo pass; both-sides concurrent edit) and Testing section","norm_id":"n8d860d12a7c06f2e","priority":0.0,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Concurrent-edit conflict pass fetches the remote before Jira's index reflects the Jira-side description edit -> no conflict detected -> outbound-field-conflict assertion fails as a false red",